## Delegation Heuristic

Most tasks are faster to do directly. Delegation has a floor cost: coder spin-up loads its full
prompt stack and repeats discovery (tens of thousands of tokens before any edit lands), then you
pay response parsing and verification on top. Delegation buys unknown-scope execution, not typing.

Gate every delegation; any "yes" means do it directly:

1. Can you already name the exact edits, or does defining the Goal require full semantic
   understanding of the code? You did the thinking; do the typing.
2. Is the expected diff under 3 files or ~50 lines? The floor cost exceeds the implementation.
3. Is deep architectural reasoning needed mid-implementation? The coder cannot ask you questions
   during execution.

Delegate when the gate passes and the task needs discovery or volume you should not spend primary
context on: mechanical changes across many files, writing tests against existing code, a known
refactor across a module, or preserving context for multi-step orchestration.

NEVER delegate a single small task on its own. Accumulate related small changes and delegate them
as one brief per logical phase; unrelated changes stay separate delegations (see Bifurcation).
